  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA warm front has lifted north of the area today, allowing for\ntemperatures to climb into the upper 70s to mid 80s with\ndewpoints increasing into the 60s. These conditions will prime\nthe area for widespread shower development ahead and along a\ncold front that is expected to sag southeast across the area\ntonight into Friday. This robust surface front will be enhanced\nwith strong mid- and upper-level energy as a trough extends\nacross much of the contiguous US. A strong upper level jet\npushing over the region coupled with mid-level vort max will be\nthe primary synoptic forcing with strong low-level frontogenetic\nforcing. The showers should develop into a line and push east \novernight resulting in a widespread 0.5 to 1 inch of \nprecipitation. Given the dry conditions recently, no flooding is\nexpected. In addition, the thunderstorm potential remains low \ngiven the unfavorable timing, but cannot be ruled out given the \nstrong synoptic forcing. Finally, gusty southwest winds ahead \nof the front will back to northwesterly behind the front. This \nstrong boundary will result in highs only climbing into the low \nto mid 60s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nBehind the departing cold front on Friday, a Canadian high pressure \nwill push east over the Great Lakes region and bring with it much \ncooler temperatures and dry conditions. There is expected to be a \nweak cold front pushing east on Sunday, however limited forcing and \nmoisture should keep conditions dry across the area. On Monday, a \ndominant high returns and will persist into at least the middle of \nthe week. This pattern will allow for dry conditions beginning \nFriday night to persist into the middle of next week. High \ntemperatures will climb into the 60s before some highs in the \n50s creep into NWPA early next week. Overnight lows will be in \nthe upper 30s to low 40s. May need to monitor for the potential \nof frost next week.",
